   <section class='publication-abstract'><h2 class='publication-abstract-heading'>Abstract</h2><div class='publication-abstract-body'>Gradient-based attribution provides input-specific information about model
parameters, but computing it via backpropagation for many samples is
expensive, while static importance scores discard input dependence. We derive
KPSA (Kernelized Parameter Sensitivity Atlas) from a block decomposition of
the empirical neural tangent kernel. It measures normalized parameter-group
sensitivity on a fixed source set and functional, embeds those sources
in an explicit query space, and uses a lightweight kernel to estimate
sensitivities for unseen inputs. We characterize query-space fit through target
stability and derive bounds that separate finite-source from irreducible
representation error and give margin-based top-<span class='mathjax-inline'>\\(k\\)</span> recovery. This paper studies
two interpretability applications. Firstly, KPSA identifies parameter groups
with high susceptibility to perturbations across vision, forecasting, and
protein-modeling setups. Across a fivefold range of parameter budgets
(0.02–0.1%), its selected groups attain 39–85% of the perturbation susceptibility
of a first-order backpropagation oracle. In vision and forecasting, these
parameter groups form sparse sensitivity circuits that extend across depth
and vary systematically with the input. Secondly, we use KPSA on an
activation-attribution problem for MLP features in transformers. It retains 81% of
the gap between a backpropagation oracle and a constant-sensitivity baseline on
vision while increasing batched query-time throughput from 551 to 2,041
images/s and reducing peak GPU memory from 4.55 to 0.44 GB. On
the protein-modeling task, it recovers 68% of this gap. An ablation of
KPSA’s kernel aggregation shows that nearest-neighbor retrieval in KPSA
space can remain competitive. The empirics show that compact kernel
atlases can store useful input-dependent parameter sensitivity while
amortizing repeated gradient attribution, a step toward scalable conditional
interpretability.</div></section>
